# Data Governance Framework

A data governance framework covers several interconnected domains:

---

## Core Components

### Data Ownership & Stewardship
- Assigning data owners (accountable) and data stewards (responsible day-to-day)
- Defining roles across business and IT
- Establishing a Data Governance Council or committee

### Policies & Standards
- Data definitions and business glossary
- Naming conventions and classification standards
- Retention, archival, and disposal policies
- Regulatory compliance policies (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX, etc.)

### Data Quality
- Accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness metrics
- Data profiling and validation rules
- Issue tracking and remediation workflows

### Metadata Management
- Business metadata (what the data means)
- Technical metadata (lineage, schema, location)
- Operational metadata (usage, access logs)
- Data catalog as the central discovery layer

### Data Security & Access Control
- Classification tiers (public, internal, confidential, restricted)
- Role-based and attribute-based access control
- Masking, encryption, and anonymization standards
- Audit trails

### Data Lineage & Provenance
- Tracking data origin and transformation history
- Impact analysis (what breaks if X changes)
- End-to-end pipeline visibility

### Master Data Management (MDM)
- Single source of truth for critical entities (customers, products, etc.)
- Deduplication and golden record creation

---

## Operational Layer

### Processes
- Issue escalation paths
- Change management for schema/data model changes
- Data request and access provisioning workflows

### Tooling
- Data catalog (Collibra, Alation, AWS Glue Catalog)
- Lineage tools (OpenLineage, Marquez)
- Quality tools (Great Expectations, dbt tests)
- Monitoring and observability

### Metrics & Reporting
- Data quality scorecards
- Governance adoption KPIs
- SLA compliance tracking

---

## Maturity Progression

Most organizations move through these stages:

| Stage | Name | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| 1 | **Reactive** | No formal governance, firefighting |
| 2 | **Defined** | Policies documented, roles assigned |
| 3 | **Managed** | Tooling in place, metrics tracked |
| 4 | **Optimized** | Automated enforcement, continuous improvement |

---

## Entry Point Guidance

The right entry point depends on your biggest pain point:

- **Regulatory risk** → prioritize security and access control first
- **Analytics maturity issues** → prioritize data quality and catalog first
